      "snippet": {
        "text": "The previous section encapsulated the key themes highlighted in the literature regarding ChatGPT's application in education. Topics such as enhanced personalisation and support in learning, accessibility, and inclusivity along with the previously mentioned challenges to academic integrity and ethical matters, are relevant to mathematics education as well. Nonetheless, specific considerations exist that are unique to the teaching of mathematics. Frieder et al. (2023) analysed ChatGPT's mathematical abilities and found that, despite enthusiasm, it often fails to consistently deliver high-quality proofs or calculations. They noted that while ChatGPT lacks the specialised performance of task-specific AI models, it offers greater versatility across domains. Similarly, Plevris et al. (2023) observed that while ChatGPT can solve basic algebra and simple logic puzzles, its reliability diminishes with complex questions, often providing inconsistent answers -a concern echoed by Remoto (2024). Despite these limitations, ChatGPT shows promise as an educational tool. Matzakos et al. (2023) found that when combined with Computer Algebra Systems, ChatGPT enhances teaching and learning by offering dynamic, interactive, and personalised educational interactions, surpassing standalone tools in creating a robust learning environment. Li et al. (2023) and Thị Hải Yến et al. ( 2023) examined ChatGPT's efficacy among junior high students in Taiwan and high school students in Vietnam, respectively. Both studies acknowledge its utility in providing detailed problem-solving guidance and enhancing understanding. Li et al. found that ChatGPT facilitates selfregulated learning in flipped classrooms, covering most curriculum areas except visually dependent topics. Meanwhile, Thị Hải Yến et al. highlighted its promise and limitations in complex subjects like derivatives and spatial geometry. Both conclude that while ChatGPT enhances understanding, it should complement, not replace, traditional teaching. \n\nPatero (2023) demonstrated ChatGPT's positive impact on student engagement and motivation among Year 9 to Year 12 students and educators. Integrated as an auxiliary learning tool, ChatGPT provided personalised assistance, interactive problem-solving, and conceptual explanations. Educators were trained to incorporate it effectively. The study found that ChatGPT improved students' perceptions of mathematics, enhancing engagement and motivation, and led to notable advancements in academic achievements.",
