      "snippet": {
        "text": "In response, recent work has attempted to align LLMs toward more Socratic, dialogic, and in-structionally coherent tutoring behaviors through prompting, fine-tuning, and new training objectives. Favero et al. (2024) fine-and prompt-tune small LLaMA-2 models to implement structured Socratic questioning, improving critical thinking, self-reflection, and perspective-taking. The Socratic Playground for Learning (SPL) shows how extensive prompt engineering can steer GPT-4 into generating multi-turn Socratic dialogues that support vocabulary, grammar, and sentence construction (Zhang et al., 2024). SocraticLM introduces a \"thought-provoking\" teaching paradigm aimed at emulating teacher-like steering toward deeper reasoning (Liu et al., 2024). TeachLM, trained on roughly 100,000 hours of one-on-one tutoring interactions using PEFT, increases student talk time, diversifies questioning strategies, and produces around 50% more dialogue turns than base models, approaching human tutor norms (Perczel et al., 2025). In programming education, Ross et al. (2025) show that GuideLM provides more Socratic guidance and more concise feedback than GPT-4o, with a small trade-off in correctness.",
