      "snippet": {
        "text": "The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into mathematics education has been rapidly evolving. Digital math learning platforms, used by millions of students every day, have shown positive results. Computer-based tutors that detect student errors and provide immediate feedback have demonstrated increased student success [68][69][70], one example of these is ASSITment, which has been developed to provide adaptive feedback and support student learning without relying on large language models (LLMs) or structured misconception datasets [71]. These technologies are now candidates for AI integration, particularly through LLM. LLMs have shown significant potential in math education, with classroom implementations already underway [19]. They improve the automated scoring of math questions, achieving human-like agreement in some cases [21,22,25,30,72]. LLMs generate diverse educational materials, including learning objectives, worked examples [22,23], teaching questions, course content [24][25][26][27][28][29], and adapted math problems at various difficulty levels [37,38]. Research demonstrates their potential to improve learning outcomes through teacher coaching [30][31][32], student support [33,34], and potentially doubling learning outcomes in AI-assisted tutoring [73]. LLMs have shown promise in the generation of math content [35,36], including automated distractor and feedback generation for multiple-choice questions [74]. They've outperformed existing approaches in algebra error classification [75]. However, LLMs still struggle to identify specific math misconceptions [76,77], generate answers that demonstrate misconceptions [76], or explain incorrect answers. with new research on automating misconception identification in student explanations showing potential [33]. There is a need for these systems to adapt to different levels of math understanding for equitable education access [20]. Large math datasets with student interactions, such as CIMA [39] and MathDial [40], are crucial to train AI systems. However, synthetic datasets can result in lower pedagogical quality responses [41,42].",
