      "snippet": {
        "text": "Large Language Models for Education Large language models (LLMs) have been widely applied to various educational tasks, including automated feedback (Guo et al. 2024;Nair et al. 2024;Zhang et al. 2025;Du et al. 2024), learning resource recommendation (Zaiane 2002;Li et al. 2024;Shahzad et al. 2025;Abu-Rasheed, Weber, and Fathi 2024;Chu et al. 2025), and conversational teaching assistance (Hu and Wang 2024;Mithun et al. 2025;Zha et al. 2024;Wu et al. 2024b). Mathematics education has emerged as a particularly active domain for LLM-based solutions (Mitra et al. 2024;Swan et al. 2023;Yan et al. 2024). Yan et al. (2025) present MathAgent, a Mixture-of-Math-Agent framework for K-12 scenarios that performs multimodal error detection and converts visual inputs into formal expressions. Wu et al. (2023a) introduce MathChat, which frames mathematical problem solving as a dialogue between a chatoriented LLM agent and a tool-using user agent, enabling iterative reasoning with tool calls. Lei et al. (2024) analyze the limitations of single LLMs on complex mathematical logical deduction and propose a multi-agent mechanism that partitions reasoning roles to improve solution quality. Related efforts span other domains as well, including physics, chemistry, and biology (Ma et al. 2024;M. Bran et al. 2024;Huang et al. 2024). In contrast to these general deployments, our work focuses on a personalized mathematics tutor that incorporates student-specific dynamics to guide practice and review. \n\nPersonalized Large Language Model Tutoring Personalized LLM tutoring adapts instruction to individual learners by modeling cognitive, affective, and preference-based dimensions. Existing methods coordinate interaction, reflection, and reaction components through memory modules and meta-agents (Chen et al. 2024;Wang, Lee, and Mutlu 2025), maintaining structured student profiles that encode knowledge proficiency, comprehension levels, and misconceptions (Liu et al. 2024a,b).",
