      "snippet": {
        "text": "LLMs for Education. LLMs are increasingly used in education for tasks like grading [6,17], question generation [32,52], and tutoring [41]. Early systems like Codex and ChatGPT showed that LLMs can help students across STEM and language learning by offering contextual feedback and answers [7,23,47]. More recent research explores using LLMs as conversational tutors [37]. However, most existing evaluations rely on limited benchmarks or informal studies, often focusing on narrow skills like math or reading [14,22]. \n\nBenchmarking LLMs. Many benchmarks test LLMs on general or domain-specific reasoning tasks, evaluating their factual knowledge and reasoning abilities. Some, like MathVista [25,34] and ScienceQA [46,56], include images or structured data, but often use synthetic problems or cover narrow domains. Our benchmark, ESTBOOK, is grounded in real standardized exams and spans multiple formats (e.g., multi-choice, text completion), providing a more realistic test of LLMs as educational agents in a heterogeneous problem-solving environment. \n\nEliciting LLM Reasoning. Improving LLM reasoning through prompting has become an emerging subject. Techniques like In-Context Learning (ICL) [20,51], Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [10,43], and Tree-of-Thought (ToT) [54,55] guide models to generate step-by-step answers and improve performance on tasks like math and logic. Yet, these methods are mostly tested on clean, synthetic datasets [4,40]. We evaluate LLMs on ESTBOOK and show their limitations in real EST questions. Additionally, we break down problem-solving steps based on question structure and offer insights about LLMs eligibility on each isolated reasoning step. \n\n3 ESTBOOK: Benchmarking English Standardized Tests Modalities. ESTBOOK captures the structural and cognitive diversity among several modalities: text (T), math symbols (S), images (I), tables (Tb), and audio (A). These modalities reflect the multimodal nature of real-world ESTs, where students are required not only to process textual information but also to interpret mathematical expressions and visual data.",
