        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Much of the existing literature on applications of generative AI in mathematics education focuses on lower-division or computationally oriented courses-like precalculus, calculus, or linear algebra (e.g., Zhuang, 2025, Torres-Peña et al., 2024, Gouia-Zarrad and Gunn, 2024, Garcia et al., 2025, Fardian et al., 2025). In these environments and even more elementary levels of mathematics, AI tools are typically used to support step-by-step problem solving, automated feedback, or tutoring systems. For instance, Microsoft partnered with Khan Academy to enable Khan Academy to offer K-12 educators free access to the pilot of Khanmigo for Teachers a pilot program of an AI-powered teaching assistant and the companies are exploring AI-powered math tutoring capabililties (Beatty, 2024). \n\nOf note, (Dilling and Herrmann, 2024) conducted an exploratory study that investigated ChatGPT's use by pre-service teachers for generating geometry proofs. Their findings revealed there were superficial misconceptions about the tool and highlighted the need for more targeted prompt design. More recent initiatives, such as LeanTutor, feature formally verified next-step suggestions and error diagnostics notably supporting proof learning for basic arithmetic proofs (Patel et al., 2025). While promising, these tools remain experimental and do not appear to have been evaluated in advanced undergraduate proof-based courses. Another emerging AI system, an auto-grader built for induction proofs using natural language processing, has been shown to offer rapid feedback and scoring (Zhao et al., 2025). \n\nWhile it shows early potential in improving proof practice, student trust in automated grading remains limited. \n\nAlthough generative AI continues to improve and new efforts are being made to extend its use into more advanced mathematics, current AI systems still demonstrate \"fragility\" in mathematical reasoning (Ahn et al., 2024). As a result, significant challenges remain in applying these tools within proof-based upper-level undergraduate mathematics courses.",
