      "snippet": {
        "text": "While programming remains a popular testbed for LLM-based systems [15,22,41,76,96,106,142], the scope has expanded to other subjects with structured solution space, like mathematics. For instance, Wang et al. [165] curated a benchmark (ReMath) co-developed with teachers to evaluate step-by-step math tutoring, with the aim to use LLMs to assist math tutors in remediating student mistakes. Pal Chowdhury et al. [125] proposed MWPTutor, a framework for generating math word problem hints by reformulating questions around the learner's next step. Pardos et al. [127] introduced OATutor, an open-source ITS platform to support adaptive tutoring research across multiple subjects. Building on this, Pardos and Bhandari [126] evaluated ChatGPT-generated hints within OATutor, comparing them to human tutor hints across algebra and statistics using an in-context learning approach using chain-of-thought reasoning with self-consistency decoding [167]. Their results demonstrate statistically significant learning gains from ChatGPT-generated hints. \n\nWhile most prior work on LLM-generated hints relies on in-context learning-typically through few-shot examples or chain-of-thought prompting-recent research has begun exploring two complementary directions: synthetic data generation and post-training. In math tutoring, for example, Wang et al. [164] demonstrate that LLMs can role-play teacher-student interactions to produce synthetic datasets, which can then be used to fine-tune smaller models [181] or to train tutoring policies via LLM-based reward models [43]. These post-trained smaller models models can match or even exceed the performance of state-of-the-art systems on specialized tutoring tasks. Scarlatos et al. [147] report that a Llama3-8B [53] fine-tuned with direct preference optimization [136] outperformed GPT models on several mathematics-tutoring benchmarks. However, such gains come with substantial costs. Post-training requires significant computational resources and depends heavily on high-quality, pedagogically grounded datasets.",
