      "snippet": {
        "text": "This is also reflected in the teaching practice as e-learning apps are increasingly being used in formal learning settings (e.g., as audience response systems (Kay & LeSage, 2009) or for conducting gamified factual knowledge tests (Wesseloh et al., 2020)). Nevertheless, due to the relatively high student-to-academic staff ratio (OECD, 2022) in many formal learning settings, we observe that human lecturers and tutors still struggle with providing proper individualization and feedback to learners. \n\nTo address this issue of insufficient individualization and feedback, we propose to introduce chatbots (also known as conversational agents) in formal learning settings. There exists increasing design knowledge on developing chatbots (Diederich et al., 2022;Feine et al., 2019). However, transferring it to educational settings in order to develop a digital tutor for large-scale classes is not easy. Typically, chatbots are only used for a short time (e.g., to get support with an urgent problem). In contrast to that, chatbots that are integrated into formal educational settings (like lectures) need to support learners in long-term use (e.g., a complete lecture period). We propose that they should interact like artificial tutors, and they need to provide guidance to students during the whole learning process (up to several months). Thus, more in-depth, long-term support is needed (i.e., answering knowledge questions or questioning the learners as part of formative assessment). Based on this need for design knowledge, we focus on designing a digital tutor for large-scale educational settings in this design-oriented study. Thus, we address the following research question: RQ How should chatbot-based digital tutors be designed that are suited to support learners in long-term learning processes in formal educational settings? \n\nTo address our research question, we develop and evaluate a chatbot-based digital tutor app for the given problem situation.",
