      "snippet": {
        "text": "Benchmarks have begun shifting evaluation toward pedagogical fidelity and dialogic quality. MathDial, a dataset of nearly 3,000 semi-synthetic math tutoring dialogues, encodes teacher-move taxonomies and shows that while GPT-3 is a strong solver, it remains a poor tutor; fine-tuning on MathDial improves equity, faithfulness, and reduces premature solution disclosure (Macina et al., 2023). Building on this, Maurya et al. (2025) introduce an eightdimension pedagogical evaluation framework and release MRBench, a dataset of 192 math tutoring conversations and 1,596 responses annotated across these dimensions. The BEA-2025 Shared Task operationalizes this taxonomy across five evaluation tracks, including mistake identification, mistake location, guidance, and actionability, using dialogues from MathDial and Bridge (Kochmar et al., 2025). More recently, SID (Socratic Interdisciplinary Dialogues) provides a large-scale benchmark of 10,000 tutoring turns across 48 interdisciplinary STEM projects, targeting higher-order reasoning and guided inquiry; results show that even state-of-the-art models struggle to support knowledge integration and transfer (Jiang et al., 2025). \n\nAdditional work focuses on new alignment and labeling frameworks. Dinucu-Jianu et al. (2025) propose an online reinforcement-learning approach that balances student success with \"solution leakage,\" training a 7B-parameter tutor that approaches proprietary models like LearnLM while offering explicit control over this trade-off. Petukhova and Kochmar (2025) re-annotate MathDial with eleven fine-grained pedagogical intents and show that LLMs fine-tuned on these labels produce more pedagogically aligned responses than those trained on the original four coarse categories.",
