      "snippet": {
        "text": "Because AI-generated images always look different even with the same prompts, our examples show naturally more a playful exploration of AI images than a systematic comparison. However, it is striking that in our explorative mathematical visualizations Midjourney provided us with the most interesting visualizations when it comes to interpreting mathematical content and generating corresponding images. These mathematical visualizations could be used as a starting point for interpretive discussions between teachers and students in the context of mathematics teaching and learning in the classroom. In primary school for example, generative AI could be used to create 2-3 images based on the same prompt that prominently features various geometric shapes. By examining and comparing these images, students can identify and discuss various geometric shapes, patterns, and mathematical relationships depicted within the image. This process encourages them to apply their mathematical knowledge in a new context, deepening their understanding of concepts such as symmetry, proportions, and spatial relationships. As they recognize, articulate and discuss the similarities and differences in the use of shapes and patterns, students train their mathematical communication and use of mathematical language as well as observational and analytical skills. In secondary school, generating images could be an engaging and enriching classroom activity, as it encourages students to apply their understanding by identifying and incorporating key mathematical concepts into their visualizations. As they create and analyze the image according to its mathematical and aesthetical quality, students can recognize and reflect on various aspects that emerge, reinforcing their comprehension and retention of the subject matter. Additionally, this process promotes critical thinking and argumentation and problem-solving skills, as students must consider how best to represent abstract ideas and information visually and mathematically sound. Thus, we see the potential of AI-generated mathematical visualizations being able to function as an innovative tool in mathematics classrooms, offering students a unique opportunity to interpret and analyze visual data. \n\nThe use of image-generating AI models in this article showed a glimpse of the current possibilities regarding the generation of mathematical and math-containing input. However, current AI models do not yet come close to the beautiful visual proofs of Nelsen (1993), Strick (2021), Miller andMontague (2012) or Cairns (2012) which might have a higher value for mathematics educational purposes.",
