        "text": "The search query [(\"chatbot\" OR \"generative artificial intelligence\" OR \"generative AI\" OR \"intelligent\" OR \"ChatGPT\" OR \"GenAI\" OR \"large language model\") AND (\"language\" OR \"listening\" OR \"speaking\" OR \"reading\" OR \"writing\" OR \"grammar\" OR \"vocabulary\")] in titles, abstracts, and keywords was used to select articles published from January 2014 to January 2025 in Web of Science core collection, Scopus and Pro-Quest. These databases are major database publishers (Zhang et al., 2023a(Zhang et al., , 2023b) ) to provide evidence-based scientific information and include influential educational research (Chiu et al., 2023), which have been frequently used by scholars (e.g., Li et al., 2024;Jeon et al., 2023) when conducting systematic reviews on AI in language education. Given the rapid development of educational technology, a decade is a suitable timeframe to research in this field and for AI technology, this a roughly one-decade period has witnessed dramatic advancement, and related papers increased significantly, including those on how AI supported language learning (Jeon et al., 2023;Ji et al., 2022). The search was limited to peer-reviewed articles and conference papers written in English with full text accessible in fields of education, social science, arts and humanity in three databases, resulting in 13,206 initial articles for further screening: 1,806 in Web of Science, 8,568 in Scopus and 2,832 in ProQuest. 695 duplicate articles were removed by the deduplication function of EndNote20, resulting in 12,511 articles for further screening, as shown in Fig. 2.",
