      "snippet": {
        "text": "One of the educational technologies powered by AI is intelligent tutoring systems (ITS), which could provide personal and adaptive learning experiences [15]. These AI-based tutors are designed to mimic the one-to-one experience between a student and a human tutor through personalized tutoring [1,69], adaptive learning [54], and conversational dialogue [31,65]. Over the years, ITS has been proven effective in education [39,76] and applied across various educational fields, such as language learning [74] and science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) education [29]. \n\nThe development of LLMs offered new possibilities in personalized tutoring and adaptive learning experience. For example, LLMs can generate quiz questions [55] personalized to a learner's understanding level [50]. LLMs also enabled AI-based tutors through interactive dialogue systems beyond traditional conversational agents (CAs). Schmucker et al. \n\n[68] developed Ruffle&Riley, an AI tutor framework that allows educators to import learning materials and generate tutoring scripts through conversation. Kakar et al. [37] developed a virtual teaching assistant that leverages OpenAI's \n\nChatGPT LLM [59] as well as instructor-approved learning materials, and they found that this approach maintained the benefits of CAs while suppressing some limitations of LLMs such as biases. In another study, LLM-based CAs also showed promise in fostering social interaction in online education [36], which could address the social needs from DHH learners [32,42,46]. \n\nGiven the benefit of using ITS, prior research adapted ITS for DHH learners to learn languages. Michaud et al. [53] developed an ITS for DHH learners whose native language is ASL to help teach them written English. There are also ITS designed to teach sign language [63,79]. Glasser et al. [27] proposed designs for sign language-based CAs to support DHH users. However, there is still a general lack of addressing accessibility and specific needs for CAs [23], such as the social support from the DHH communities. Additionally, there has been little work exploring how ITS could support DHH learners beyond learning language.",
